# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
The original medium severity is retained. Static evidence and the prior executable validation support a real DoS: node.rs calls full metadata replay validation in each heartbeat while holding the PG mutex; command_log.rs replays 1..=applied_log_index with SQLite command_bytes loads and CRC64 verification; compaction reports UnsupportedUntilCheckpoint; and validation observed loop hit count equal to retained entries plus increasing heartbeat time. The issue should not be raised to high/critical because exploitation requires an authenticated/write-capable client and a control-plane storage-node deployment, and the impact is runtime availability only, with no confidentiality, integrity, authentication bypass, or RCE impact.
## Likelihood
high - Once a control-plane storage-node deployment is in use, exploitation is straightforward for a write-authorized client because it uses normal S3 metadata-changing operations and the heartbeat repeats automatically. Likelihood is not high because it requires write privileges, enough request volume/time to grow the log, and a non-default control-plane heartbeat deployment; the repository default listener is localhost and no IaC shows public exposure. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - The credible impact is availability degradation of affected PGs or a storage node: periodic heartbeat work becomes O(total retained applied metadata commands), performs SQLite reads and CRC64 work, and holds the per-PG mutex. This can block normal metadata operations and cause missed leases or unavailability. There is no demonstrated data exfiltration, integrity bypass, privilege escalation, or code execution.
## Assumptions
- The affected deployment uses the storage-node or combined role with ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_SOCKET_PATH configured so the storage-node control-plane refresh/heartbeat loop runs.
- The attacker has normal S3 write capability for at least one bucket/key namespace, or an operator has intentionally enabled public write semantics for a bucket.
- The service is reachable to that client over the configured HTTP listener; the repository default is 127.0.0.1:9000 and no deployment manifests prove public internet exposure.
- Metadata-changing operations can be repeated enough times to grow retained metadata_command_log history before operational intervention.
- S3 write-authorized requester or public-write bucket
- control-plane heartbeat path enabled for storage node or combined process
- repeated metadata-changing operations to grow retained command log
## Path
S3 writer
  -> HTTP frontend
  -> metadata command application
  -> retained metadata_command_log
  -> periodic storage-node heartbeat
  -> full replay under PgStore mutex
  -> CPU/IO exhaustion + PG operation blocking
