- `crates/storage/src/pg_store/command_log.rs:2249-2258` - Metadata command-log compaction without checkpoints is unsupported, leaving retained entries unbounded by this compaction path.
## Narrative
The finding is real for control-plane storage-node deployments. The code obtains a per-PG PgStore mutex, then heartbeat proof generation calls validate_metadata_command_replay_state_preserving_pending_slot. That validator loops over every applied metadata log index, loads command_bytes from SQLite, verifies checksums/hashes, and compaction currently reports UnsupportedUntilCheckpoint. Normal metadata writes record commands into the same log. The prior executable validation confirmed the replay loop runs once per retained entry during one heartbeat and showed heartbeat time increasing with retained entries. This is an availability issue, not a confidentiality/integrity bypass or code execution issue.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication before normal request dispatch
- Coordinator bucket/object authorization and ACL checks
- Global max_connections and max_inflight_requests configuration
- Default ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is 127.0.0.1:9000
- Storage-node and control-plane communication uses configured Unix sockets
- TLS is optional via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
## Blindspots
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker, or other deployment manifests were present to prove actual public exposure, load balancer type, or production process role.
- The prior validation exercised a Rust unit-test/control-plane heartbeat path, not a complete HTTP-to-storage end-to-end exploit in this stage.
- Static analysis cannot determine real operator rate limits, filesystem performance, retained log sizes, or whether deployments use the default legacy-local role versus control-plane storage-node roles.
- Cloud IAM/service-account posture is unknown because repository-only analysis was required and no cloud APIs were called.
